  "worked_solution": "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z=-2(-\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z=-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$",
